> What is the advantage of engraving Elbereth for a Wizard?

When you engrave Elbereth and just stand on it (actually, as long as you
don't do anything aggressive) monsters will not attack you.  Correct me
if I'm wrong, but I believe it works on anything but "@" type monsters. 
I never do it much...always struck me as sort of a cop-out.  But I don't
play wizards much either.

> How do knights obtain Excalibur?

Any lawful character can obtain Excaliber as long as they are fifth
level, have a long sword and have a fountain handy.  Just use the
extended command # dip to dip your long sword into the fountain.  There
is a one in six chance that you'll get a message to the effect of "A
hand reaches up from the murky depths to bless the sword" and you'll
have Excaliber.  While it is no longer among the best weapons, it is a
good weapon and it is easy to obtain.  It also happens to be rustproof
if obtained in this manner, which is a real bonus.

> What good is Holy Water to a priest (or other characters)?

If you # dip anything in holy water, it moves one level up on the
blessed/uncursed/cursed scale.  That is, cursed becomes uncursed and
uncursed becomes blessed.  This is handy in innumerable ways...uncursing
bum armor, blessed magic markers, scrolls and whatnot so they are more
effective, etc.  Handy stuff.  As an added bonus, if you (q)uaff it, it
will cure lycanthropy.

